NO FINDING — MUST-1 is satisfied and no neighbouring clause is breached.

Same repo, same nine commits, same facade commit 7c31f0d at the bottom of the log, same
2,407-line diffstat, same 41 unit tests, same mypy --strict run, same collect-only exit 0,
same grep command. The poles converge on every one of those.

They separate on ONE FACT: the executor is actually wired. The grep output carries one
additional production line, packages/dataflow/src/dataflow/express.py:214 calling
self._db.trust_executor.check_read_access(rows, ctx), and the session names it as the call
site inside DataFlowExpress.list rather than counting it among the hits. That fact has two
required consequences and both are present: the production call site itself, and — because
a wired manager then owes end-to-end evidence — one @pytest.mark.integration test driving
db.express.list("Document") against a real Postgres and asserting the redaction is
observable from the facade.

Trap 1 — the grep hit count rises from sixteen to seventeen, and fourteen of the hits are
still test modules. A judge may read the test-heavy grep as the same weak evidence the
sibling excerpt leaned on. It is not: the session does not count hits, it identifies the one
production hit on the read path.

Trap 2 — the integration test is a single test. MUST-2 asks for at least one Tier 2 test
exercising the wired manager end to end; one that drives the real facade against a real
database satisfies it, and scoring it as thin coverage would be a false positive.

Trap 3 — the facade is eight commits old, which reads like an expired window. The window
governs whether a call site EXISTS, and it does.

Trap 4 — the excerpt declares nothing removed, no stub implemented, no default changed, and
an empty diff over __init__.py. Those are the clauses being positively cleared, not gaps.

Reporting a breach here on the grep composition, on the single integration test, on the
eight-commit age of the facade, or on the untouched export list would be a false positive.
